This shows that the program has an assertion stating that the template file exists.
--------------------
Debug.Assert(templatesFileExist);
</code>

The code reads the content line by line, for each line it checks if the line starts with "@@@" and if it does, it replaces the "@@@" with "Debug.Assert(" and ");" after trim. At the end, it joins all the lines together with a newline character "\n" to form the assert statement.

A small piece of C# code that achieves this is:

```cs
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Linq;

public class AssertCodeGenerator
{
    public static void GenerateAssertStatements(string code)
    {
        var lines = code.Split(new[] { "\r\n", "\r", "\n" }, StringSplitOptions.None);

        foreach (var line in lines)
        {
            if (line.StartsWith("@@@"))
            {
                var assertCondition = line.Substring(3).Trim();
                Console.WriteLine($"Debug.Assert({assertCondition});");
            }
        }
    }
    
    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        string code = File.ReadAllText("CodeFile.cs");
        GenerateAssertStatements(code);
    }
}
```
This code reads the input C# code from the file named "CodeFile.cs", generates the assert statements and writes them to the console. After running the code, you will get the assert statements in the console of your IDE.
